This is a common issue with PHP sessions. The problem occurs because all browser tabs share the same session cookie (usually `PHPSESSID`), so when you call `session_destroy()` in one tab, it destroys the session for all tabs.

Here are several solutions to prevent this:

## Solution 1: Use Different Session Names for Different Applications

If you're running different web applications, give each one a unique session name:

```php
// Application A
session_name('APP_A_SESSION');
session_start();

// Application B
session_name('APP_B_SESSION');
session_start();
```

## Solution 2: Use Session Namespaces

Create separate session namespaces within the same session:

```php
// Application A
session_start();
$_SESSION['app_a'] = [
    'user_id' => 123,
    'logged_in' => true
];

// Application B
session_start();
$_SESSION['app_b'] = [
    'user_id' => 456,
    'logged_in' => true
];

// Destroy only app_a session data
unset($_SESSION['app_a']);
```

## Solution 3: Implement Tab-Specific Sessions

Create a unique session identifier for each tab:

```php
// On page load
session_start();

// Generate or retrieve tab ID
$tab_id = $_GET['tab_id'] ?? uniqid('tab_', true);

// Store session data under tab-specific key
$_SESSION['tabs'][$tab_id] = [
    'user_data' => $yourData,
    'last_activity' => time()
];

// When destroying session for specific tab
function destroyTabSession($tab_id) {
    session_start();
    unset($_SESSION['tabs'][$tab_id]);

    // Clean up empty tabs array
    if (empty($_SESSION['tabs'])) {
        unset($_SESSION['tabs']);
    }
}
```

